YOUR DEVICE on this node: rk3588-t3. RK3588 big.LITTLE — MUST pin (taskset) to clusters; an unpinned run is meaningless.

== PHASE 1 — DEVICE BENCHMARK (docs/DEVICE_RUNBOOK.md, bead ob-8ms.3) — do once, re-run only if a number looks off ==
1. Build the static binaries: `./scripts/build_device_bench.sh`  (native gcc is present; outputs dist/bench_gdn_*). The script builds several ISA variants — RUN only the one(s) for your core; others may illegal-instruction.
2. Set the CPU governor to performance for valid numbers (needs sudo, password 'cell'):
     `for c in /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpufreq/scaling_governor; do echo cell | sudo -S tee "$c" >/dev/null; done`
   Record which governor you used. Read thermals before & after: `cat /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone*/temp`.
3. Capture the run(s) to CSV:
   # RK3588: confirm big=cpu4-7 (higher max freq) then PIN to each cluster:
   taskset -c 4-7 ./dist/bench_gdn_armv8.2dot --repeats 30 --csv > results/raw/rk3588-t3_big.csv   # A76 bigs
   taskset -c 0-3 ./dist/bench_gdn_armv8a     --repeats 30 --csv > results/raw/rk3588-t3_little.csv # A55 littles
   (First do a human-readable eyeball: `<binary> --repeats 30` .)
4. Capture provenance: `python3 bench/manifest.py > results/manifests/rk3588-t3.json`  (stdlib, degrades gracefully).
5. Commit & push:
   `mkdir -p results/raw results/manifests && git add results/ && git commit -m "t3: device-fleet bench rk3588-t3 (ob-8ms.3)" && git push -u origin bench/t3`
6. Compare achieved GiB/s vs the device's spec bandwidth (DEVICE_RUNBOOK "What we are actually testing"). If surprising (e.g. Jetson beats Pi5 on the scan kernel), append a short note to docs/FINDINGS.md and commit.

== PHASE 2 — PORTABLE CODE (loop forever) ==
1. `bd dolt pull`            # sync teammates' beads
2. `bd ready`                # choose OPEN, UNBLOCKED, NON external-gate work; prefer label "portable"
3. `bd update <id> --claim`  # claim atomically so collaborators don't duplicate
4. Implement per the issue + PLAN.md + BEADS.md. Run tests; `bd preflight`.
5. `git add -A && git commit -m "t3: <what>" && git push origin bench/t3`
6. Close: `bd close <id> --reason "Landed in <commit>"`.
   - NEVER close a benchmark bead without a committed run manifest.
   - Every `decision`-type bead must produce an ADR in docs/adr/ before it closes.
7. `bd export -o .beads/issues.jsonl && git add .beads/issues.jsonl && git commit -m "beads: <change>" && git push`
8. `bd dolt push`            # publish the live beads DB for teammates
Repeat: pull -> ready -> claim -> implement -> test -> commit/push -> close -> export -> dolt push.

== PRs ==
Keep ONE open PR from bench/t3 -> main: `gh pr create --base main --head bench/t3 --title "t3 device bench + work" --body "..."` (or `gh pr edit` to update). Do NOT merge it yourself — a reviewer merges on an hourly loop.

== RULES ==
- Read PLAN.md before your first claim (two-track Physical/Edge AI structure, Aug 14 deadline).
- external-gate beads are NOT actionable by effort; prefer "portable" work.
- Record durable insights with `bd remember "..."`.
- Never benchmark under QEMU. A number without a manifest is not a result (PLAN.md §9).
HARD RULES: push only to bench/t3; never force-push or rewrite main; never close a bead you didn't complete; avoid `bd edit`/`bd create-form` (interactive — they hang).
